+.TH LGAMMA_R 3 "June 2026" "Substrate" "Library Functions Manual"
+.SH NAME
+lgamma_r, lgammaf_r, lgammal_r \- log-gamma function (reentrant)
+.SH LIBRARY
+Math library (libm, \-lm)
+.SH SYNOPSIS
+.B #include <math.h>
+.PP
+.BI "double lgamma_r(double " x ", int *" signp );
+.br
+.BI "float lgammaf_r(float " x ", int *" signp );
+.br
+.BI "long double lgammal_r(long double " x ", int *" signp );
+.PP
+Feature-test macro requirements (see
+.BR feature_test_macros (7)):
+.PP
+.RS 4
+.BR lgamma_r (),
+.BR lgammaf_r (),
+.BR lgammal_r ():
+.RS 4
+_GNU_SOURCE || _DEFAULT_SOURCE
+.RE
+.RE
+.SH DESCRIPTION
+The
+.BR lgamma_r ()
+function returns the natural logarithm of the absolute value of the
+Gamma function of
+.IR x :
+.PP
+.RS 4
+.IR ln|Gamma( x )| .
+.RE
+.PP
+It is a reentrant variant of
+.BR lgamma (3).
+Where
+.BR lgamma (3)
+records the sign of
+.IR Gamma( x )
+in the global variable
+.BR signgam ,
+.BR lgamma_r ()
+stores that sign (+1 or \-1) in the
+.B int
+pointed to by
+.IR signp
+and
+.B does not
+read or modify
+.BR signgam .
+This makes it safe to call from multiple threads concurrently.  If
+.I signp
+is NULL, the sign is not stored.
+.PP
+The
+.B f
+and
+.B l
+suffixed variants
+.RB ( lgammaf_r (),
+.BR lgammal_r ())
+take and return
+.B float
+and
+.B "long double"
+values respectively.
+.BR lgammal_r ()
+is computed directly in 80-bit precision rather than by widening a
+.B double
+result.
+.PP
+To obtain the Gamma function itself rather than its logarithm, use
+.BR tgamma (3).
+.SH RETURN VALUE
+On success, these functions return
+.IR ln|Gamma( x )| ,
+and (when
+.I signp
+is non-NULL)
+.I *signp
+is set to +1 if
+.IR Gamma( x )
+is positive and \-1 if it is negative.
+.PP
+If
+.I x
+is a non-positive integer (a pole of the Gamma function),
+.B \(+-HUGE_VAL
+(or
+.BR \(+-HUGE_VALF ,
+.BR \(+-HUGE_VALL )
+is returned.
+.PP
+If
+.I x
+is \(+-0,
+.B +HUGE_VAL
+is returned.
+.PP
+If
+.I x
+is large enough that the result overflows,
+.B \(+-HUGE_VAL
+is returned.
+.PP
+If
+.I x
+is \(+-infinity,
+.B +infinity
+is returned.
+.PP
+If
+.I x
+is a NaN, a NaN is returned.
+.SH ERRORS
+See
+.BR math_error (7)
+for information on how to determine whether an error has occurred when
+calling these functions.
+.PP
+The following errors can occur:
+.TP
+Pole error:
+.I x
+is 0 or a negative integer
+.B \(+-HUGE_VAL
+is returned and a divide-by-zero floating-point exception
+.RB ( FE_DIVBYZERO )
+is raised.  If
+.I "math_errhandling & MATH_ERRNO"
+is non-zero,
+.I errno
+is set to
+.BR ERANGE .
+.TP
+Range error: result overflow
+.I x
+is too large.
+.B \(+-HUGE_VAL
+is returned.  An overflow floating-point exception
+.RB ( FE_OVERFLOW )
+is raised.  If
+.I "math_errhandling & MATH_ERRNO"
+is non-zero,
+.I errno
+is set to
+.BR ERANGE .
+.SH ATTRIBUTES
+For an explanation of the terms used in this section, see
+.BR attributes (7).
+.TS
+allbox;
+lb lb lb
+l l l.
+Interface	Attribute	Value
+T{
+.BR lgamma_r (),
+.BR lgammaf_r (),
+.BR lgammal_r ()
+T}	Thread safety	MT-Safe
+.TE
+.PP
+Unlike
+.BR lgamma (3),
+which is
+.B MT-Unsafe
+because of its use of the global
+.BR signgam ,
+the
+.BR _r
+variants are
+.BR MT-Safe :
+they communicate the sign exclusively through the caller-supplied
+.IR signp .
+.SH STANDARDS
+None.  These are glibc / 4.3BSD-Reno extensions.
+.SH HISTORY
+The reentrant gamma functions originate in 4.3BSD-Reno and are provided
+as a GNU C library extension.
+.SH SEE ALSO
+.BR lgamma (3),
+.BR tgamma (3),
+.BR math_error (7)
+.SH COLOPHON
+This page is part of the Substrate operating system documentation.
